==== Wave 2: Mechanics

===== Turbofan Power

+ *N1 / N2* — the fan and core compressor speeds: the power gauges of a turbofan. Thrust follows N1; manage it with the thrust levers and let the auto-throttle (where fitted) hold the target. The Challenger's engines are managed largely by their computers — your job is to set the target and watch the limits.
+ *EGT / temperature limits* — exhaust gas temperature is the one gauge you must never exceed for long: hot days, high power, and low climb speeds push it. Watch it like the stall warning of Wave 0.
+ *Thrust management* — takeoff, climb, and cruise thrust are set by target N1 computed from the conditions; the FMS/performance pages give you the numbers. Smooth thrust changes are the mark of the professional.

===== Pressurization & Cabin

+ *The system* — bleed air from the engines keeps the cabin at a comfortable altitude while the aircraft climbs high. Set the cabin altitude target and the controller climbs/descents the cabin gently.
+ *The climb/descent rule* — climb and descend at the aircraft's cabin-rate limit, not just the flight-path one: the clients feel the cabin rate long before the aircraft rate.
+ *Descent planning* — the business jet descends fast; plan the top of descent so the cabin pressure and the flight path arrive at the airport together.

===== FMS & Navigation

+ *The avionics suite* — the Challenger's flight deck (a Pro Line 21-style suite) pairs a full FMS with big displays: load the plan (exported from LittleNavMap), add the SID/STAR and the approach, and let the box fly lateral and vertical guidance.
+ *RNAV/ILS approaches* — from the RNAV/GPS approach with its vertical guidance to the raw ILS localizer/glideslope: know both, fly both, brief both from the plate in AviTab.
+ *Holds & missed approaches* — the racetrack and the escape: programmed in the FMS, flown by hand when it is not. Practice them; the airline waves assume you can.

===== IFR Discipline

+ *The instrument scan* — attitude, airspeed, altitude, heading, vertical speed, cross-checked against the approach plate's profile. In cloud, the scan is your life.
+ *Minimums* — every approach has decision altitudes/heights and visibility minimums; below minimums, the go-around is not an option, it is the procedure.
+ *Radio work* — readbacks, position reports, and the quiet confidence of someone who files and flies the system: the sim's ATC plus the radio basics from Wave 0, at IFR pace.
+ *Anti-ice & de-icing* — engine and wing heat, and the rule that ice is a *leave* condition, not a *push-through* condition: plan the escape before you enter.

===== Workload Management

+ *Aviate, navigate, communicate* — in that order. When the workload spikes, the airplane and the path come first; the radio waits.
+ *The checklist flow* — flows and checklists: do the flow from memory, confirm with the list. Wave 2 is where this habit becomes permanent.
+ *Single-pilot triage* — delegate to the systems that earn it (autopilot, FMS) and keep the decisions that are yours (weather, fuel, minimums, go-around).
